[Music]  growing up is tough and two local  organizations are stepping in to help  with our city's young men and women and  to join us more is to tell us more is  paul krakowicz the nevada region  president of intermouth and health  intermountain healthcare and liz  ortenberger the ceo of safeness welcome  you two thank you so much for joining us  thank you for having us liz i was just  talking to you off the air about this  these are two great programs really  helping our young adults  turn into really  big important members of this community  tell us a little bit about the program  absolutely so both programs are designed  to help high school athletes both male  and female navigate leadership and their  responsibilities because we know a  couple of things we know that 95 percent  of c-suite executives were athletes oh  interesting right and we know that  athletics can that relationship that  athletes have with their coaches is one  of the most important and dynamic  relationships they have as young people  so if we can help elevate that space by  talking about the things around healthy  relationships and what affect us growing  up and why it's inappropriate to say you  throw like a girl or to body shame in  the locker room we can elevate that  space for everybody so this one of the  programs is coaching boys into men and  then the other is athletes as leaders  yes tell us about the difference between  the two so coaching boys into men is  that as the title says is is really the  the boy version or the young athlete  version for the males and the athletes  as leaders is the girl a portion of the  program so both programs have the same  end result which is elevating athletes  with all of the right information that  they need to navigate their world what a  great great idea paul tell us a little  bit about intermountain healthcare's  role in these programs so as a  not-for-profit we look for where we can  have an impact in the community and  really around a chief health need we  realize that domestic violence was a big  issue  coaching boys into men and athletes as  leaders are both naturally recognizing  they use evidence-based care to do this  and finding a partnership with safe nest  was just kind of a perfect blend for us  to be able to bring together into the  community and what have you guys seen as  you work with these kids how how they've  grown what are how rewarding must that  be so absolutely so actually democracy  prep which is one of the schools that  we're heavily involved with had an  incident where a football player cat  called at a girl  and her parents actually called the  school and complained and the coach  who's trained in this program said  normally i would have not really known  how to navigate this but because of this  program i knew the conversation i needed  to have in my locker room and i knew how  to help my young men navigate this world  tell us how people can get involved how  do people  help out with this program absolutely so  if you are at a charter school you can  call safe nest you can call our main  line and get information or go to  safeness.org and request us to come out  to your school if you're in clark county  school district we need you to be asking  your administrators to get this program  active so that we can come to your  schools and implement we have lots of  football coaches who want this program  but we haven't been able to get ccsd to  approve it quite yet and that is so  important paul thank you and  intermountain healthcare for being a  part of these just a great initiative  and thank you for sponsoring this  segment for more information about  safeness visit safeness.org

